{
  "dom_id": "step-q109199",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14120089/assets/f4bb6636c104cf17bcd4b25037a3490ba30b7c1380b152c33504d9015903bdca.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=2{sin}^{2}⁡xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2xy)\\,dx+2{sin}^{2}⁡(xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)-(-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy)\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2xy).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
